Server Component (the default — no directive needed):

// app/projects/page.tsx - no 'use client', so this is a Server Component
async function ProjectsPage() {
  const projects = await getProjectsFromDatabase(); // can fetch data
                                                       // DIRECTLY, even
                                                       // including server-
                                                       // only secrets, since
                                                       // this code never
                                                       // reaches the browser
  return (
    <ul>
      {projects.map((project) => <li key={project.id}>{project.name}</li>)}
    </ul>
  );
}

Client Component (explicitly opted into, only when genuinely needed):

'use client';
import { useState } from 'react';

function LikeButton() {
  const [liked, setLiked] = useState(false); // useState REQUIRES the browser
  return <button onClick={() => setLiked(!liked)}>{liked ? 'Liked' : 'Like'}</button>;
}

Rule of thumb: use a Server Component by default; add 'use client' specifically when a component genuinely needs useState, useEffect, event handlers, or any browser-only API.

