Fetching directly inside an async Server Component:

async function ProjectsPage() {
  const response = await fetch('https://api.example.com/projects');
  const projects = await response.json();

  return (
    <ul>
      {projects.map((project: Project) => <li key={project.id}>{project.name}</li>)}
    </ul>
  );
}

Next.js extends the standard fetch() API (the exact same fetch from Module 4, lesson 9) with built-in caching options:

await fetch(url, { cache: 'force-cache' });  // cache indefinitely (the default)
await fetch(url, { cache: 'no-store' });     // never cache, always fetch fresh
await fetch(url, { next: { revalidate: 60 } }); // cache, but refresh after 60 seconds

For data that genuinely changes per-request or per-user (Module 9's authenticated data, for instance), { cache: 'no-store' } ensures every request gets genuinely fresh data, directly avoiding a stale, incorrectly-cached response being served to the wrong user.

